WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.180
Last spring.

00:00:21.181 --> 00:00:25.150
Only one thing seems certain: After a year plus of being socially distance, single people everywhere, were going to hit the streets and the sheets like some mixture of the premenopausal Sex

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.079
and the C

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.910
ity girls and the randy castaways of Love Island. Everywhere you looked, experts and reporters were predicting a slutty summer, a hot vax summer, an eruption of raw carnal desire not seen since the heyday of the sexual revolution.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.419
There's a bar on every single corner

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.319
in New York City.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.089
Not everybody is an alcoholic.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:55.259
I mean, just because things are

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.269
available, people don't change who

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.219
they are. And what this pandemic

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.049
did was it just made all

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.999
these singles grow up.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.499
They are really now looking for

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.339
something stable.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.529
I mean, yes, look still counts,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.712
but not the way they did count.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.289
Anthropologist Helen Fisher is the

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.329
chief science adviser to the dating

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.399
site Match.com, a senior

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.229
fellow at the Kinsey Institute

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.149
and one of the authors of Singles in

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.279
America, an annual survey

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.259
of 5'000 representative

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.359
people of all genders, races,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.519
ages and sexual orientations.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.529
Now in its 11th year, Singles

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.269
in America has charted the growth of

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.339
what Fisher calls "slow love",

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.529
or an extended vetting process

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.899
in which people, especially women,

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.939
are far more choosy, deliberate

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.099
and intentional in picking romantic

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.019
partners. The hot vax summer

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.999
didn't happen, says Fisher,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.619
because for decades we've been

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.479
trending away from bad boys

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.309
and crazy girls and more

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.959
toward stable, serious

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.039
relationships.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.349
There's less and less sex going

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.359
on, and I do think

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.219
it's because the rise of

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.109
all these people dedicated to their

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.149
career. It's amazing how Americans

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.709
are so dedicated to their career.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:08.849
There's many more women in the

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.719
workforce.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.429
I'm calling them the new Victorians.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.979
I mean, they have much less sex than

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.259
we did in my generation.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:17.549
Fisher says that, as women have
gained equality

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.349
and education in the workplace,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:20.939
they've started to exercise more

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.649
discretion when it comes to pairing

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.179
off and whether or not to have

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:24.719
children.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.219
This year in the Singles in America

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.419
study, when we asked, Are you

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:32.919
.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.229
We were the picky sex.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.999
The Singles in America survey finds

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.739
that men overwhelmingly want a

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.899
partner with an equal or superior

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:40.799
career.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.299
That's the so-called "Clooney

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.279
Effect," named for the Oscar winning

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.169
actor, whose wife Amal is

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.789
a highly regarded human rights

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.499
lawyer. In other words, a more

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.359
libertarian world in which men and

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.859
women have more options due to

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.719
dating apps and more equality

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.197
due to social and economic changes

00:02:56.198 --> 00:02:58.409
turns out to be a lot less

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.519
libertine.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.319
Women are stuck in the home, men

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.029
aren't stuck with being the sole

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.099
responsibility for the good economic

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.329
health of the family.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.219
We live in a world now where you can

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.199
really be who you want

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.209
to be. You can jump class, get

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.039
yourself an education, move into

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.229
the job market at a different level.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.369
I mean, this is all good.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.869
We're living longer.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.159
I mean, we're living longer.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.839
It's a more exciting time than any

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:22.860
time on the planet.

